<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles I of Anjou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1226–1285</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Charles of Anjou</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles I of Anjou was the youngest brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-ix-of-france">Louis IX of France</a>, Count of Anjou and of Provence through his marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beatrice-of-provence">Beatrice of Provence</a> in 1246. Invited by the pope, he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> and took the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> in 1266. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">Sicilian Vespers</a> of 1282 lost him the island to Aragon, leaving his heirs the mainland Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou.jpg" alt="Charles I of Anjou" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Son of Groucho from Scotland · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Some_Old_Guy_2_(5642308121)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
